es classes rares »."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"aa9aaa87","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011693,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.550964+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.539271+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 3.2 — Séparer les features (X) et la cible (y)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e654c658","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012065,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.574913+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.562848+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["On range d'un côté les 14 colonnes d'entrée (X), de l'autre la colonne à prédire (y)."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":13,"id":"99d64b6b","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.600307Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.599463Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.605955Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.605241Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.020791,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.607513+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.586722+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Forme de X : (1281, 14)\n","Forme de y : (1281,)\n"]}],"source":["X = df_clean.drop(columns=[\"Diagnosis\"])   # tout sauf Diagnosis\n","y = df_clean[\"Diagnosis\"]                   # uniquement Diagnosis\n","\n","print(\"Forme de X :\", X.shape)   # (1281, 14)\n","print(\"Forme de y :\", y.shape)   # (1281,)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"392a9538","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011589,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.630865+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.619276+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 3.3 — Encoder la cible (texte → nombres)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"196016bf","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011128,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.654365+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.643237+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Les modèles ne comprennent pas le texte (\"Leukemia\"…). On transforme chaque nom de classe en un numéro avec LabelEncoder."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":14,"id":"cadf0969","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.679205Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.678745Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.853149Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.852237Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.188745,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.854827+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.666082+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["0 -> Healthy\n","1 -> Iron deficiency anemia\n","2 -> Leukemia\n","3 -> Leukemia with thrombocytopenia\n","4 -> Macrocytic anemia\n","5 -> Normocytic hypochromic anemia\n","6 -> Normocytic normochromic anemia\n","7 -> Other microcytic anemia\n","8 -> Thrombocytopenia\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n","\n","le = LabelEncoder()\n","y_encoded = le.fit_transform(y)   # ex : \"Healthy\"->1, \"Leukemia\"->2, ...\n","\n","# Pour retrouver la correspondance nom <-> numero :\n","for i, nom in enumerate(le.classes_):\n","    print(i, \"->\", nom)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"7389ab0d","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012109,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.879662+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.867553+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 3.4 — Découper en train / test, puis normaliser"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"5dd32ace","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011528,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.903028+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.8915+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Pourquoi un découpage ? On entraîne le modèle sur une partie (train, 80%) et on teste sur une partie qu'il n'a jamais vue (test, 20%). C'est ce qui mesure sa vraie capacité à généraliser.\n","Pourquoi stratify ? Vu le déséquilibre, on force le test à contenir la même proportion de chaque classe que l'ensemble complet."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":15,"id":"b729758a","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.927704Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.927163Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.040362Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.039378Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.127662,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.042255+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:25.914593+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Train : (1024, 14) | Test : (257, 14)\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n","from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n","\n","X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n","    X, y_encoded,\n","    test_size=0.2,        # 20% pour le test\n","    random_state=42,      # pour des resultats reproductibles\n","    stratify=y_encoded    # garde les proportions des classes \n","    #Vu le déséquilibre, on force le test à contenir la même proportion de chaque classe que l'ensemble complet.\n",")\n","\n","# Normalisation : mettre toutes les variables a la meme echelle\n","scaler = StandardScaler()\n","X_train_scaled = scaler.fit_transform(X_train)   # on APPREND sur le train\n","X_test_scaled  = scaler.transform(X_test)        # on APPLIQUE au test\n","\n","print(\"Train :\", X_train_scaled.shape, \"| Test :\", X_test_scaled.shape)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"7273a697","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011218,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.067322+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.056104+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Le point crucial à comprendre (et à expliquer) : on fait *fit_transform* uniquement sur le train, puis transform sur le test. Pourquoi ? Pour ne pas « tricher » : le modèle ne doit rien apprendre des données de test, même pas leur moyenne. C'est l'erreur classique qu'on appelle data leakage (fuite de données).\n","Pourquoi normaliser ? K-NN calcule des distances : si une variable va de 0 à 1000 (PLT) et une autre de 0 à 1 (PCT), la première écrase tout. La normalisation met tout le monde sur la même échelle. C'est indispensable pour K-NN et la régression logistique."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ae991489","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011208,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.089646+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.078438+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# **Étape 4 — Entraînement des modèles (Régression logistique, K-NN, Arbre de décision, Random Forest)**"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ad21be2c","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011387,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.113444+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.102057+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 4.1 — Régression logistique"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1867a0b7","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011698,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.13715+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.125452+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Principe : malgré son nom, c'est un algorithme de classification. Elle calcule la probabilité qu'un patient appartienne à chaque classe et choisit la plus probable. Elle cherche une frontière (linéaire) entre les classes."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":16,"id":"44493693","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.162365Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.161474Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.307491Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.30607Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.161486,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.310457+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.148971+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Regression logistique - accuracy test : 0.852\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n","\n","log_reg = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)   # max_iter eleve pour bien converger\n","log_reg.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)          # entrainement\n","acc_log = log_reg.score(X_test_scaled, y_test)\n","print(\"Regression logistique - accuracy test :\", round(acc_log, 3))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"8fef9ab0","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.02245,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.357881+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.335431+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 4.2 — K-NN (K plus proches voisins)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"6debee98","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.02246,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.402873+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.380413+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Principe : pour classer un nouveau patient, on regarde ses k voisins les plus proches dans les données d'entraînement et on lui donne la classe majoritaire parmi eux. On commence avec k=5."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":17,"id":"40d8a3f3","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.442851Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.441974Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.605223Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.604226Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.181812,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.606954+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.425142+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["K-NN (k=5) - accuracy test : 0.77\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier\n","\n","knn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5)\n","knn.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)\n","acc_knn = knn.score(X_test_scaled, y_test)\n","print(\"K-NN (k=5) - accuracy test :\", round(acc_knn, 3))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"997a7bfe","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011969,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.631415+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.619446+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 4.3 — Arbre de décision"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"be0554f7","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012048,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.655378+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.64333+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Principe : une série de questions « si... alors... » sur les variables (ex : si HGB < 11 et MCV < 80 alors...), qui divise les données en groupes de plus en plus purs jusqu'aux feuilles."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":18,"id":"c35fa36b","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.680994Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.680481Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.733299Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.732377Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.067704,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.73491+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.667206+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Arbre de decision - accuracy test : 0.996\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n","\n","tree = DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=42)\n","tree.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)\n","acc_tree = tree.score(X_test_scaled, y_test)\n","print(\"Arbre de decision - accuracy test :\", round(acc_tree, 3))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"76ebe2ee","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012099,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.759237+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.747138+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 4.4 — Random Forest"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1cffcd2e","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012556,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.784104+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.771548+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Principe : une forêt de nombreux arbres de décision, chacun entraîné sur une partie aléatoire des données. La prédiction finale est le vote majoritaire de tous les arbres. C'est une technique d'ensemble (le bagging vu en cours), généralement plus robuste qu'un seul arbre."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":19,"id":"bb1c4176","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.811316Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.810777Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.228125Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.227355Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.432428,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.229981+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:26.797553+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Random Forest - accuracy test : 0.981\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n","\n","forest = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, random_state=42)\n","forest.fit(X_train_scaled, y_train)\n","acc_forest = forest.score(X_test_scaled, y_test)\n","print(\"Random Forest - accuracy test :\", round(acc_forest, 3))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"d64caf24","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011984,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.255319+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.243335+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Première lecture :\n","\n","L'arbre de décision atteint 99.6% et Random Forest 98.1% — excellents.\n","La régression logistique (85%) est moins bonne : sa frontière linéaire est trop simple pour bien séparer 9 classes.\n","K-NN (77%) est le plus faible ici.\n","\n","⚠️ Mais attention  un arbre de décision seul qui atteint 99.6%, c'est suspect. Un arbre non limité (max_depth non fixé) a tendance au surapprentissage (overfitting) : il apprend les données par cœur, y compris le bruit. Il faudra vérifier ça à l'étape suivante.\n","Surtout, avec un dataset déséquilibré, l'accuracy seule est trompeuse : un modèle peut avoir une bonne accuracy globale tout en ratant complètement les classes rares (Leukemia, Macrocytic…). C'est pour ça qu'à l'Étape 5 on regardera :\n","\n","la matrice de confusion (qui se trompe avec quoi),\n","la précision / rappel / F1-score par classe,\n","une validation croisée pour confirmer que les scores sont fiables et pas un coup de chance du découpage.\n","\n","\n","Résumé de l'Étape 4 \n","Les 4 modèles du cours sont entraînés. Les modèles à base d'arbres dominent largement les modèles linéaires/distance sur ce problème. Mais les scores très élevés (99.6%) et le déséquilibre des classes imposent une évaluation approfondie avant de conclure — l'accuracy seule ne suffit pas.\n","On passe à l'Étape 5 — Évaluation et comparaison (matrices de confusion, F1-score, validation croisée) ?"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e19e8afe","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012418,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.279551+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.267133+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# **Étape 5 — Évaluation et comparaison (matrices de confusion, F1-score, validation croisée**"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"6a4d7532","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012219,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.303715+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.291496+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["On attaque l'Étape 5 — Évaluation approfondie. C'est ici qu'on vérifie si nos scores sont vraiment bons ou trompeurs. Trois outils : validation croisée, rapport de classification (F1), matrice de confusion."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"917fd8f4","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012179,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.327826+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.315647+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 5.1 — Validation croisée (le score est-il fiable ?)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"b9ec31a0","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011976,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.351893+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.339917+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Principe : au lieu de mesurer sur un seul découpage train/test (qui peut être chanceux), on découpe les données en 5 morceaux, on entraîne 5 fois, et on regarde la moyenne et la stabilité des scores. Si les 5 scores se ressemblent → le modèle est fiable."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":20,"id":"dfe300bf","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.378751Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.378237Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.935675Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.934725Z"},"papermill":{"duration":1.57292,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.937398+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:27.364478+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Reg. logistique    : 0.855 (+/- 0.028)\n","K-NN               : 0.767 (+/- 0.015)\n","Arbre              : 0.989 (+/- 0.007)\n","Random Forest      : 0.978 (+/- 0.007)\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\n","\n","for nom, modele in [(\"Reg. logistique\", log_reg), (\"K-NN\", knn),\n","                    (\"Arbre\", tree), (\"Random Forest\", forest)]:\n","    scores = cross_val_score(modele, X_train_scaled, y_train, cv=5)\n","    print(f\"{nom:18s} : {scores.mean():.3f} (+/- {scores.std():.3f})\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4b7272e0","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011933,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.963025+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.951092+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Interprétation : les scores en validation croisée confirment ceux du test, et le « +/- » est petit (≤ 0.028) → les modèles sont stables, pas un coup de chance. L'arbre (0.989) et Random Forest (0.978) restent en tête. Le score élevé de l'arbre se confirme donc — il n'est pas dû à un découpage chanceux."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"2e94c0da","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011845,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.986875+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.97503+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 5.2 — Rapport de classification (précision, rappel, F1 par classe)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"51f63013","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011752,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.010159+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:28.998407+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Pourquoi c'est crucial ici : avec le déséquilibre, on veut voir si les classes rares sont bien prédites. Trois mesures :\n","\n","Précision : quand le modèle dit « classe X », a-t-il raison ? \\\n","Rappel : parmi les vrais « classe X », combien sont retrouvés ?\\\n","F1-score : moyenne des deux (la mesure clé en cas de déséquilibre)."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":21,"id":"7af45cdf","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.044446Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.044011Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.080016Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.079002Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.055523,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.082349+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.026826+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["                                precision    recall  f1-score   support\n","\n","                       Healthy       0.97      1.00      0.99        67\n","        Iron deficiency anemia       0.97      0.97      0.97        38\n","                      Leukemia       0.89      0.89      0.89         9\n","Leukemia with thrombocytopenia       1.00      0.50      0.67         2\n","             Macrocytic anemia       1.00      1.00      1.00         4\n"," Normocytic hypochromic anemia       0.98      1.00      0.99        56\n","Normocytic normochromic anemia       1.00      1.00      1.00        54\n","       Other microcytic anemia       1.00      0.92      0.96        12\n","              Thrombocytopenia       1.00      0.93      0.97        15\n","\n","                      accuracy                           0.98       257\n","                     macro avg       0.98      0.91      0.94       257\n","                  weighted avg       0.98      0.98      0.98       257\n","\n"]}],"source":["from sklearn.metrics import classification_report\n","\n","y_pred = forest.predict(X_test_scaled)\n","print(classification_report(y_test, y_pred, target_names=le.classes_))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"3157b9e4","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.013134,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.116364+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.10323+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Comparons l'arbre et Random Forest sur les classes rares :\n","\n","Interprétation — c'est ici qu'on voit la vérité cachée par l'accuracy :\n","\n","La plupart des classes ont un F1 ≈ 0.97–1.00 → excellent.\n","MAIS regarde « Leukemia with thrombocytopenia » : rappel = 0.50, F1 = 0.67. Le modèle n'en retrouve qu'1 sur 2 ! Pourquoi ? Parce qu'il n'y a que 2 patients de cette classe dans le test (colonne support) et 11 en tout. Trop peu pour bien apprendre.\n","\n","C'est LE point à souligner : « l'accuracy globale (98%) masque une faiblesse sur les classes très rares ; le macro avg du F1 (0.94) — qui traite toutes les classes à égalité — est plus honnête que l'accuracy »."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ed7fdd39","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.011888,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.140229+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.128341+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 5.3 — Matrice de confusion (qui se trompe avec quoi)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"44e023c3","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012021,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.164327+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.152306+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Principe : un tableau qui croise vraies classes (lignes) et classes prédites (colonnes). La diagonale = les bonnes réponses ; tout ce qui est hors diagonale = les erreurs."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":22,"id":"149f5464","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.190352Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.189779Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.490756Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.489856Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.316031,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.492671+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.17664+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 640x480 with 2 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, ConfusionMatrixDisplay\n","\n","cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, y_pred)\n","disp = ConfusionMatrixDisplay(cm, display_labels=le.classes_)\n","disp.plot(xticks_rotation=90, cmap=\"Blues\")\n","plt.title(\"Matrice de confusion - Random Forest\")\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"ac2277bd","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.012896,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.519506+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.50661+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Interprétation : la diagonale est très foncée → presque tout est bien classé. Les rares erreurs (hors diagonale) :\n","\n","1 « Leukemia with thrombocytopenia » prédit comme « Leukemia » (les deux sont liées médicalement, erreur compréhensible),\n","1 « Iron deficiency » → « Normocytic hypochromic », 1 « Other microcytic » → « Iron deficiency » (anémies microcytaires proches),\n","1 « Leukemia » et 1 « Thrombocytopenia » → « Healthy »."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"005ad483","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.013134,"end_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.545424+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-08-15T00:53:29.53229+00:0